Why are we doing this if we're removing AI?

Because criticality-aware routing does not require AI.

Our old concept would have been something like:

Patient data
    ↓
ML model
    ↓
Predicted criticality

We've removed that.

Now it is:

Patient/application data
        ↓
Explicit deterministic rules
        ↓
Traffic class

That means the logic is transparent and reproducible.

Your teacher can literally look at:

if heart_rate > 140 or spo2 < 90:
    return "CRITICAL"

and understand exactly why the traffic was classified that way.

Again, the specific numbers here are prototype networking thresholds, not a medical diagnosis system.

What happens to these classes?

This becomes much more interesting in Step 20.

Suppose the network has:

Path A:
low delay
some congestion

Path B:
higher delay
low congestion

For ordinary traffic, we might use one configurable engineering policy, while critical traffic might place more weight on delay, jitter and packet loss.

For example, conceptually:

NORMAL
  ↓
balanced QoS weights

URGENT
  ↓
stronger reliability/latency emphasis

CRITICAL
  ↓
strongest latency/loss/jitter emphasis

So Step 20 will connect:

$$ \text{Criticality} \rightarrow \text{QoS weights} \rightarrow \text{Cost} \rightarrow \text{Path} $$

That's what makes the project healthcare-aware SDN routing, rather than just generic shortest-path routing.

